EBS vs EFS — Not all AWS storage is the same.

Imagine this
You're working on two different AWS projects:

Project 1: A backend app with MySQL
You launch an EC2 instance.
You install MySQL.
You want fast, local, disk-level storage that sticks even if the instance restarts.
You use EBS — Elastic Block Store.

Why?
It’s like plugging a hard disk directly into your laptop.
Fast. Private. And only your laptop (EC2) can use it at that time.
Perfect for:
       Databases
       Boot volumes
       Logs
      Any single-instance storage


Project 2: A web app running on 5 EC2s
All EC2s need to access the same files — maybe logs, images, configs.
You can’t keep syncing them manually.
You use EFS — Elastic File System.

Why?
It’s like a shared Google Drive — all machines can open, read, and write together.
Fully managed, auto-scaling, no headache.
Perfect for:
     Shared file access
     Content management systems
     Scalable web apps
     Multi-AZ workloads


The Core Difference?
EBS is like a personal pen drive — fast, but for one device.
EFS is like a shared folder on the cloud — slower, but everyone can use it.


You don’t pick what's “cool” —
You pick what fits your architecture.
Because in cloud, smart choices save time, money, and headaches.
